    <description>ALF-RS-12 (LFRDN-342), per the user&apos;s direction: once a Handover reaches AF_Status__c = Completed (all 8 documents done, set by AF_FL_Handover_UpdateChecklistGate), auto-progresses the Opportunity toward Closed Won. Two-step, not a direct jump: AF_VR_Opp_SequentialStageProgression blocks any stage move of more than 1 step, and this org&apos;s stage model has &quot;Take the Keys&quot; between Commit and Closed Won (its own gate requires invoice balance = 0) - confirmed via live testing (2026-09-16) that a direct Commit-to-Closed-Won update is rejected. So: if the Opportunity is at Commit, this flow first advances it to Take the Keys (matches the BRD&apos;s own language - handover confirmed means delivered/keys handed over); once at Take the Keys, if the Invoice Summary is Fully Paid, it continues in the same transaction to Closed Won, routed through the existing AF_Opportunity_ClosedWonPaymentGate Apex check (the same one the manual Closed Won path already enforces) rather than setting the stage directly, so there is one single source of truth for the Closed Won conditions. If the Opportunity is below Commit when the Handover completes, this flow does nothing (that is a real data-sequencing problem, not something to paper over by forcing the stage forward). After-save (not before-save) because before-save Flow cannot call Apex Actions.

Also sets Opportunity.AF_DeliveryConfirmation__c = true in the same update as StageName going to Closed Won. Discovered via live testing that AF_VR_Opp_ClosedWonGate (a separate declarative backstop, LFRDN-341) independently requires this field true and it was never set by anything in the org - it is documented elsewhere as reserved for a future Keyloop/MuleSoft delivery-status sync that was never built, meaning Closed Won was unreachable by ANY path, not just this one. A completed Handover with a generated Delivery Note is real local proof of delivery, so this flow now supplies it until the Keyloop sync exists; if that integration is later built, it should take over writing this field and this assignment should be reconsidered.</description>
